{"records":[{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2bsmmfny76","title":"The Cys/N-degron pathway mediates secretory autophagy under oxidative stress","description":"Leaderless cytokines lacking signal peptides are dominantly secreted as first-line responses to cellular stresses. However, the molecular mechanisms governing their secretion remain murky. Here, we report that leaderless cytokines are released by secretory autophagy, whose amphisome biogenesis is mediated by the Cys/N-degron pathway during oxidative stress. The Nt-Cysox degron of cytokines undergoes ATE1-dependent Nt-arginylation and binds the ZZ domain of the autophagic cargo receptor/N-recognin p62/SQSTM1. Such interaction conformationally and biologically activates p62 into oligomeric complexes with the multivesicular body (MVB) transmembrane protein CD63, whose topology inversion mediates phagophore-MVB fusion for amphisome biogenesis. Peptidomimetic p62 antagonists were developed to inhibit secretory autophagy of pro-inflammatory cytokines in models of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Our results implicate the Cys/N-degron pathway in amphisome biogenesis for paracrine inflammatory signaling.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-23T07:25:23.701Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2bsmmfny76","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2bsmmfny76"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Su Bin","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ji Su","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Su Jin","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hye Yeon","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Minje","last_name":"Kang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Min Ju","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Do Hyun","last_name":"Han","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Inflammation","Secretory Pathway","Autophagy","Exosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"p47zrwrxct","title":"Disrupting BRD4-BD1–p62 Engagement Restores Protective Autophagy","description":"Protective autophagy is profoundly impaired in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF), yet no therapies directly restore this fundamental cellular defense mechanism. Here, we identify the BRD4-BD1-p62 interface as a druggable regulator of protective autophagy and develop SKLB-39b, a BRD4-BD1-selective inhibitor that disrupts this interaction. By engaging Leu92 and Ile146, SKLB-39b restores ULK1-dependent autophagy, suppresses fibroblast activation and epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and attenuates pulmonary fibrosis with improved tolerability relative to pan-BET inhibition in the mouse studies performed here. These findings establish a mechanistic framework for domain-selective BET inhibition and demonstrate how disrupting a disease-associated protein-protein interaction within an epigenetic reader can restore protective autophagy. More broadly, this work identifies the BRD4-BD1-p62 interface as a therapeutic opportunity for autophagy-deficient fibrotic diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-19T09:10:26.098Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p47zrwrxct","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p47zrwrxct"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jifa","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yu","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chengyong","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juncheng","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Doudou","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yao","last_name":"Xiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Binghan","last_name":"Xue","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cailing","last_name":"Gan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuxi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tinghong","last_name":"Ye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Ouyang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vtvxykv2cr","title":"2020_Cell reports resubmission tables","description":"All tables associated with our paper titled \"High throughput CRISPR screening identifies genes involved in macrophage viability and inflammatory pathways\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-15T08:44:50.23Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vtvxykv2cr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vtvxykv2cr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Susan","last_name":"Carpenter","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Macrophage","Immunity","Innate Immunity","Screening","Pooled Analysis","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"53j3vrhddj","title":"Opposing regulation of the STING pathway in hepatic stellate cells by NBR1 and p62 determines the progression of hepatocellular carcinoma","description":"Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) emerges from chronic inflammation to which activation of hepatic stellate cells (HSCs) contributes by shaping a pro-tumorigenic microenvironment. Key to this process is p62, whose inactivation leads to enhanced hepatocarcinogenesis. Here, we show that while p62 positively regulates STING ubiquitination by TRIM32 by displacing NBR1, which results in the activation of the interferon (IFN) cascade, NBR1 prevents TRIM32 interaction with and the activation of STING, leading to impaired IFN synthesis. NBR1 also antagonizes STING function by promoting its trafficking from the Golgi to the endosome-lysosomal degradative cascade independent of autophagy. Importantly, NBR1 deletion completely reverts the tumor-promoting function of p62-deficient HSCs. The upregulation of the STING-IFN pathway by NBR1 deficiency enhances the anti-tumor response mediated by CD8+ T cells. These results identify NBR1 as a synthetic vulnerability of p62-deficiency in HSCs. NBR1 loss, by promoting the STING/IFN pathway, boosts anti-tumor CD8+ T cell responses to restrain HCC.\nNbr1 knock-out and wild-type hepatic stellate cells were profiled in three biological replicates under normal conditions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-20T18:33:10.351Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/53j3vrhddj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/53j3vrhddj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Moscat","last_name":"Diaz-Meco","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Interferon","Hepatocellular Carcinoma","Hepatic Stellate Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2c97jkvx9c","title":"UFMylation maintains YAP stability to promote vascular endothelial cell senescence ","description":"Endothelial cell senescence is an accomplice for vascular aging, which leads to cardiovascular diseases (CVD). Evidences showed that Hippo- Yes-associated protein (YAP) signaling pathway plays an essential role in aging-associated CVD. However, the exact role of YAP protein in endothelial cell senescence remains not fully clear. Here, we reported that YAP was elevated in senescent human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVECs) and inhibition of YAP by either specific siRNAs or inhibitor Verteporfin could attenuate HUVECs senescence. In contrast, overexpression of YAP induces cell senescence. Besides, we found that UFMylation activity and YAP were both elevated in senescent cells. Mechanistically, we found that UFMylation, a newly identified ubiquitin-like modification with essential biological functions, maintains the stability of YAP and further found that YAP is a substrate for UFMylation. Importantly, we found that compound 8.5, an inhibitor of E1 of UFMylation, can attenuate cell senescence with reduced YAP protein and alleviate vascular aging and improve cardiac function in aged mice. Therefore, compound 8.5 may be a potential small molecule for delaying vascular aging. Together, our finding provides molecular mechanism by which UFMylation maintains YAP stability and exerts an important role in promoting cell senescence, and identified that a previously unrecognized UFMylation is a potential therapeutic target for anti-aging. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-29T07:58:34.298Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2c97jkvx9c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2c97jkvx9c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yanan","last_name":"liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Aging","Vascular Injury","Vascular Endothelium"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5z7ppzwpcw","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 screening of the deubiquitinase subfamily identifies USP49 as a protein stabilizer of PAX9 and MSX1 regulating odontogenesis","description":"Deubiquitination of proteins by DUBs has emerged as a key regulator of protein stability and has been shown to be critically involved in signal transduction cascades. However, the function and regulation of individual DUBs are not well understood at the molecular level. Several screening approaches to identify DUBs and their target protein substrates in diverse cellular functions have been reported previously. For example, shRNA/siRNA libraries targeting different DUBs or overexpression of ectopically introduced DUBs have been used to investigate the impact of DUB function on target substrate protein stability. These conventional methods have several limitations, such as the limitation of RNAi or shRNA-based methods to transcripts, and these techniques often result in partial repression or restoration of gene functions in a few generations, with temporary effects.  The results obtained from these methods may not be accurate and might lead to misinterpretations of the data. To overcome this, we developed a loss-of-function study based on individually generated single cell-derived biallelic DUB knockout clones in mammalian cells and provide an alternate and improved model to explore the molecular mechanism of DUBs.\n\nIn this study, we used CRISPR/Cas9 (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats/CRISPR-associated) mediated DUB knockout screening kit based on genome-scale knockout of the entire set of genes that encode human ubiquitin-specific proteases (USPs), which are the largest subfamily of DUBs (>50 genes), and then we systematically applied the panel to screen DUB candidates that might regulate PAX9 and MSX1 proteins. PAX9 and MSX1 are critical for the transition in odontogenic potential from epithelium to mesenchyme. Although genetic mutations in PAX9 and MSX1 cause tooth agenesis, the regulation of PAX9 and MSX1 protein levels by deubiquitinating enzymes (DUBs) might be a crucial factor during tooth development. Our screening system identified USP49 as a key regulator of PAX9 and MSX1 protein stability. USP49 interacts and deubiquitinates PAX9 and MSX1 protein and upregulates several odontogenic transcription factors in human dental pulp stem cells (hDPSCs). In animal models, USP49 depletion was correlated with downregulation of Pax9 and Msx1 protein expression resulting in several morphological defects in calcified tissues such as reduced dentin growth, reduced enamel space, abnormal enamel formation, and irregular mineralization. Thus, the depletion of USP49 leads to an inadequate transition from bud to cap stage in the early stages of tooth development. This novel finding suggests that the USP49-PAX9-MSX1 axis could serve as a prognostic marker and novel regulatory target in odontogenesis and can serve as a catalyst for bioengineering tooth implant research to improve clinical care.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-08T12:30:21.375Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5z7ppzwpcw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5z7ppzwpcw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Suresh","last_name":"Ramakrishna","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","Stem Cell","Animal Development","Tooth Enamel","Tooth Development"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7vvt3g3dyc","title":"SQSTM1 promotes selective autophagic degradation of multiple PRRSV proteins via recruiting E3 ligase TRAF6 to inhibit viral replication","description":"Porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome (PRRS) is an economically significant disease affecting the global porcine industry, caused by porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus (PRRSV). Although it has been reported that PRRSV can exploit autophagy to facilitate its own replication, the role of selective autophagy during PRRSV replication remains largely unknown. The present study showed that the autophagic cargo receptor SQSTM1 is a newly identified host restrictive factor against PRRSV. Overexpression of SQSTM1 promoted the autophagic degradation of PRRSV Nsp5, Nsp12, GP4, and GP5 proteins. Subsequently, GP4 and GP5 was used as the target proteins to investigate the mechanism underlying the degradation of PRRSV proteins by SQSTM1. Overexpression of SQSTM1 promoted the autophagy adaptor protein LC3 to recruit GP4, as well as its transport to LAMP2-positive lysosomal compartments for subsequent autophagy-lysosomal-mediated degradation. SQSTM1 interacts with both GP4 and GP5 proteins, and promotes the polyubiquitination of GP4 and GP5 by recruiting the host E3 ubiquitin ligase TNF receptor-associated factor 6 (TRAF6). Further analysis revealed that TRAF6 catalyzes the K63-linked, but not K48-linked polyubiquitination of GP4 at K115 and of GP5 at K59, which are indispensable for their interaction with SQSTM1 and subsequent degradation. Knockdown of TRAF6 abolished SQSTM1-mediated degradation of Nsp5, Nsp12, GP4, and GP5, as well as the anti-PRRSV activity, implying a synergistic effect of SQSTM1 and TRAF6 in degrading PRRSV proteins and suppressing viral replication. The present study is the first to demonstrate that selective autophagy plays an important role in host defense against PRRSV infection, which provides new insights for the development of anti-PRRSV drugs or vaccines.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-08T20:11:57.457Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7vvt3g3dyc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7vvt3g3dyc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Angke","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Animal Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":138814,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":130,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":138814,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":138202,"restricted":66,"embargoed":546},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2327},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1392},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":658},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":476},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":307},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":273}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":136164},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1908},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":136},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":898},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":829},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":516},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}